1. assignment is a statement:     r = (b.x = 5)         -> SyntaxError: cannot assign to attribute here. Maybe you meant '==' instead of '='? ; after b.x = 5, b.x is 5
2. the setter's value directly:   Box.x.fset(b, 6)      -> 'ignored' ; setattr(b, 'x', 7) -> None
3. a property with only fset:     w.w = 3 stores 3 ; reading w.w -> AttributeError
4. two values on the right:       b.x = 1, 2; b.x       -> (1, 2) (a tuple)
5. inside the class, self is required: b.set_wrong(50)  -> 50 , b.x still (1, 2) ; b.set_right(60) -> 60 , b.x now 60
6. chained assignment:            a = b.x = 9           -> a is 9 , b.x is 9 (allowed as a statement)
7. __setitem__ too:               b['k'] = 'v'; b['k']  -> 'v' ; b.__setitem__('k2', 'v2') -> 'ignored_too'
8. += goes through the pair:      b.x += 1; b.x         -> 10 (getter, then setter; the statement has no value)
9. a universal hook:              __setattr__ sees every assignment: (__setattr__ saw color = 'red') ; sorted(vars(logged)) -> ['color']
